{
  "title": "Nested multicalls revert, breaking composability and availability",
  "body": "## Finding Description\n`Covenant` exposes a payable `multicall()` that runs an array of batched calls via `delegatecall` (through `MulticallLib.multicall()`), and enforces a post-batch balance equality check to prevent over/under-deposits of ETH.\n\nAffected function code:\n\n[Covenant.sol#L507-L524](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-10-covenant/blob/d5ebe4461564b46cacf8a90cf11add29470ef001/src/Covenant.sol#L507-L524)\n\n```solidity\nfunction multicall(bytes[] calldata data) external payable returns (bytes[] memory results) {\n    // runs in the context of this call, inherits msg.value\n    uint256 balanceBeforeCall = address(this).balance - msg.value;\n\n    // perform multicall via delegatecall\n    _isMulticall = true;\n    results = MulticallLib.multicall(data);\n    _isMulticall = false;\n\n    // enforce exact match\n    if (address(this).balance != balanceBeforeCall) revert Errors.E_IncorrectPayment();\n    return results;\n}\n```\n\nIn turn, `MulticallLib.multicall()` iterates the calls and performs `delegatecall` back into the same contract (so the callee sees the same execution context):\n\n[MultiCall.sol#L26-L32](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-10-covenant/blob/d5ebe4461564b46cacf8a90cf11add29470ef001/src/libraries/MultiCall.sol#L26-L32)\n```solidity\nfunction multicall(bytes[] calldata data) internal returns (bytes[] memory results) {\n    results = new bytes[](data.length);\n    for (uint256 i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {\n        results[i] = Address.functionDelegateCall(address(this), data[i]);\n    }\n    return results;\n}\n```\nRoot cause\n- The end-of-batch balance equality check is applied independently at each nesting level, but each level computes `balanceBeforeCall` using the same top-level `msg.value` because inner batches execute via `delegatecall`.\n- There is no notion of per-batch “consumed value”; thus, an inner `multicall()` assumes the entire transaction `msg.value` must be fully balanced within its own sub-batch.\n\nHighest-impact scenario\n- A nested batch where the inner `multicall()` does not itself forward any ETH, but the outer batch intends to do so later. The inner batch computes `balanceBeforeCall = (prevBalance + msg.value) - msg.value = prevBalance`, while the actual end-of-inner-batch balance remains `prevBalance + msg.value`. The equality check fails and the transaction reverts, making nested batching brittle and non-composable.\n\n## Impact\nMedium.\n- Availability/composability is impacted: nested multicalls revert even in benign cases (e.g., inner batch does nothing), breaking integrations that rely on multi-level batching and deferred ETH forwarding within the outer batch.\n\n\n## Likelihood Explanation\nHigh.\n- `delegatecall`-based multicalls are commonly composed. Any integrator nesting `multicall()` or building batch abstractions that rely on forwarding ETH later in the outer batch will hit this revert systematically.\n\n\n\n\n\n## Recommendation\nOption A: Depth-aware enforcement (preferred)\n- Track depth and only enforce the equality check at the outermost level.\n- Keep `_checkPayment()` strict only when `depth == 0`.\n\nOption B: Disallow nested multicalls (simpler, if nesting is unnecessary)\n- Fail fast when already inside a multicall."
}